# The rational prime lies in the maximal ideal of the C_p integers

Theorem statement: `PadicComplexInt.natCast_prime_mem_maximalIdeal_v2`
(`8fd9a5b3-c34b-470e-9f4c-45ed3d02c9dc`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-25.

Proof: submission `20d8ea8e-1d0d-4c0a-8aec-11efd6cb3ee2`, by davidloeffler, 2026-09-25 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

The rational prime p belongs to the maximal ideal of the valuation ring of C_p.

## Explanation of the source proof

An element of the valuation ring $\mathcal O_{\mathbf C_p}$ lies in its maximal ideal exactly when
it is not a unit. The $p$-adic valuation of the rational prime $p$ is $p^{-1}$, which is different
from $1$ because $p$ is prime. Thus $p$ is not a unit of $\mathcal O_{\mathbf C_p}$ and belongs to
its maximal ideal.

